add('lesson:'+access,'Container separates authentication factors, access-policy models and privileged access.',[
 'The profile replaces the authored title/summary. Broad4.6 and Applied are observed metadata, not calibrated difficulty, approved child scope or a complete identity architecture.',
 'The introductory objectives/aliases and rich child teaching do not approve canonical roles, independent assessments, actual PAM deployment or any prior overlapping hold.'
]);
add('keywords:'+access,'Four definitions introduce MFA, RBAC, PAM and least privilege.',[
 'Factor categories differ from the number of prompts; role membership and PAM labels do not prove least privilege. The concise least-privilege time wording is a minimization aim, not mandatory temporary elevation for every identity.',
 'All four definitions are inspected, but only the first three are correct keys in delivered practice. Least privilege appears as an alternative, not an independently assessed skill.'
]);
add('check:'+access,'Reveal-only example distinguishes JIT time limits from JEA action limits.',[
 'The stated approved hour and backup-only commands support the two distinctions. No actual session, identity approval, enforcement, revocation or recovery is tested.',
 'The UI reveals a model response; it accepts no learner answer and supplies no automated correctness, wrong-response feedback or trusted score.'
]);
add('section:'+access+':0','Distinguish independent factor categories, local activation and biometric error measures.',[
 'Password/security questions remain knowledge; a local PIN can instead unlock a possession authenticator. Risk signals alone are not substitute factors. The adjacent password-plus-PIN trap must be read with this local-activation qualification.',
 'FAR/FRR concern impostor acceptance/genuine rejection; EER/CER is a test-configuration comparison point, not a live measured assurance guarantee. Biometrics need protected fallback and anti-spoofing; no NIST AAL conformance is established.'
]);
add('section:'+access+':1','Five table rows compare owner delegation, mandatory policy, roles, attributes and condition rules.',[
 'DAC/MAC/RBAC/ABAC/rule-based examples describe decision bases, not exclusive implementation categories. Role and attribute policies can coexist; rule-based and ABAC conditions may overlap.',
 'Classic label/clearance MAC and role-based nurse examples do not establish least privilege, a medical/legal approval or that every authorization engine uses exactly one model.'
]);
add('section:'+access+':2','PAM capabilities combine controlled privilege duration/actions with emergency and recording policy.',[
 'PAM can provide vaulting, rotation, approvals, brokering and recording under configuration; JIT limits time and JEA limits permitted actions. Requesting identity and platform still need protection.',
 'Break-glass is a protected/tested/reviewed emergency path. Recordings may contain sensitive data; retention/access/privacy/tamper policy is needed. A vault or recording alone proves neither individual attribution nor a complete effective audit.'
]);
add('traps:'+access,'Three cautions distinguish knowledge prompts, role/attribute policies and shared-secret accountability.',[
 'Usually knowledge-only password plus PIN does not include the qualified local authenticator activation case. RBAC/ABAC contrast is introductory and does not deny combined policy models.',
 'Vaulted shared credentials need identity/session attribution beyond storage. No prior glossary, question, feedback or canonical-link hold is resolved by this teaching.'
]);
add('lesson:'+federation,'Container separates sign-in experience, trust domains, delegated access, identity claims and passkeys.',[
 'Profile header/summary, broad4.6 and Applied remain observed metadata. The two sections have no authored Beyond flag, but that does not approve every detailed protocol statement as precise exam scope.',
 'No real SAML/OIDC/OAuth integration, mobile sign-in, credential ceremony or production authentication implementation is approved or executed.'
]);
add('keywords:'+federation,'Five concise definitions introduce SSO, federation, OAuth, SAML and passwordless.',[
 'SSO concerns reduced repeated authentication, federation concerns trust, OAuth concerns resource access, SAML can carry authentication assertions, and passwordless omits traditional password entry. These terms do not establish identical sessions or assurance.',
 'Only SSO/federation/OAuth are correct keys in the first three delivered questions. SAML/passwordless remain reviewed definitions/alternatives; neither is independently assessed by those questions.'
]);
add('check:'+federation,'Reveal-only mobile-app example selects OIDC for standard identity claims above OAuth.',[
 'An access token alone is not the standard ID-token authentication contract. Identity-token validation and separate application authorization are still necessary; the example is not a runnable login recipe.',
 'This source-only model reveal has no response input, grading key, wrong-answer feedback or new bank-question/feedback credit.'
]);
add('section:'+federation+':0','Five table rows distinguish SSO, federation, SAML assertions, OAuth resource access and OIDC identity.',[
 'One sign-in experience need not mean every app shares a cookie, local session or permissions. Federation can cross trust domains within one organization.',
 'Signed SAML assertion is typical evidence, not a universal requirement that every assertion be individually signed; response signing/bindings and recipient/audience/freshness/trust validation matter. SAML can carry other assertion types.',
 'OAuth access/scopes do not themselves define login; OIDC adds ID tokens and authentication claims. Validate issuer/audience/time and nonce as applicable; authenticating a user does not grant every application permission.'
]);
add('section:'+federation+':1','Passkey teaching bounds public-key RP binding and phishing resistance by the full ceremony.',[
 'RP public key/authenticator-protected private key and RP-ID binding support ordinary phishing resistance in a well-implemented flow. Origin/challenge/verification checks matter; resistance is not immunity to endpoint or recovery compromise.',
 'User verification, sync/backup, recovery and policy determine assurance; not every passkey/passwordless event is MFA. Synced credentials need not be hardware-bound or non-exportable. No AAL3, every-provider or every-ceremony approval is inferred.'
]);
add('traps:'+federation,'Three cautions retain authorization/authentication, trust-domain and phishing-resistance boundaries.',[
 'OAuth and OIDC have different contracts; federation may involve same-organization domains, while SSO is the sign-in experience.',
 'Malware, weak recovery and compromised endpoints remain risks. No token validation, federation trust, passkey provider or live security posture was tested.'
]);
